WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.450
Speaker 1: All right.

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:02.720
Very big news yesterday.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.749
And we have to give praise to Congresswoman
Cory Bush for really pushing for this.

00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:13.169
The I would call this a progressive victory
of sorts, even though it may be struck down

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:15.589
by the Supreme Court relatively quickly.

00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:18.100
The CDC has extended the eviction moratorium.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.330
We talked about this last week.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:23.190
We talked about this a bunch of times because
it's a really important issue.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:28.810
The CDC has issued a new eviction ban based
on the pandemic effective through October

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.040
3rd.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.189
The former moratorium expired last month.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:36.140
As we covered, there were 11 million Americans
behind on rent.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.610
As the Delta variant suggests, this will be
targeted at areas of the country experiencing

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.670
high levels of infection from coronavirus.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:50.170
It will last 60 days until October 3rd, and
the protection could cover around 90 percent

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.829
of renters.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:54.880
Now, this may be struck down.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.930
And I think that, you know, I'm giving you
both sides of it.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.880
This is really a progressive victory.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.760
And Cory, Bush deserves a lot of credit.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.869
She was instrumental in pushing this to happen.

00:01:04.869 --> 00:01:13.340
At the same time, there is a big Supreme Court
threat that this will not hold the there is

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.539
a Politico article which calls it a potential
clash with the Supreme Court.

00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:27.170
And what's at issue here is whether the CDC
really has the authority to do this.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.060
And it may well be the answer that the Supreme
Court comes up with.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.149
Maybe no.

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:38.240
Joe Biden yesterday said he went to constitutional
scholars to find out about the CDC's legal

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:44.079
authority and what they do that is most likely
to pass muster constitutionally.

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:50.509
And Biden said the bulk of the scholarship
suggested this would not pass muster in the

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:51.529
courts.

00:01:51.529 --> 00:01:55.560
But there were some scholars that said it
could.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.890
This targeted moratorium comes after the White
House insisted for days that the CDC doesn't

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.630
have the authority to act and that Congress
must pass an extension.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:11.230
The House has left for a multi week recess
after failing to pass an extension with Pelosi

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:15.319
and other top Democrats furious at the White
House for putting this at their feet.

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:19.159
So bottom line, this may not hold.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:25.519
It appears as though the opinion of the Supreme
Court is that the CDC cannot do an eviction

00:02:25.519 --> 00:02:30.090
moratorium, that it must come from Congress
and Congress is out of session.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.770
Let's hope that's not the way it goes down.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.599
But I think we have to be prepared for the
reality that it may.

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:40.069
Of course, this is an absolutely crucial thing
to do.

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:46.549
There are people who, even in normal non pandemic
situations, can't afford an unexpected 400

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:47.549
dollar expense.

00:02:47.549 --> 00:02:49.220
Something like half the country can't.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:54.670
So if you have two, three, four or five even
more months of rent stacking up and all of

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:58.590
a sudden the protection against getting evicted
goes away, you were at real risk of getting

00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.590
evicted.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:00.769
Now, just one other thing.

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:06.799
I don't believe that the nearly the 11 million
or even eight or nine million would actually

00:03:06.799 --> 00:03:07.799
get evicted.

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:10.760
There are things taking place at the state
level.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.650
There are other programs that are available
to some of these people.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.390
So the number really at risk of getting evicted
like in the next month was nowhere near the

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.390
11 million.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.730
It doesn't change the importance and significance
of doing this.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:26.050
And now we have to see if it will hold up
in court.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.129
Let me know your thoughts.

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:29.019
You can find me on Twitter at David Pakman.

